abstract: >-
  Nonlinear spectral evolution requires control of the frequencies present
  initially and those created by interaction. We study how spectral bounds can be
  retained with the construction of a state and reused in later operations and
  observations. A declared source and its native operations determine a generated
  class under fixed admissibility rules. Finite constructions pass to limits
  through a common stronger bound and convergence in a weaker norm. For a
  nonnegative self-adjoint generator with compact resolvent we prove a Galerkin
  error estimate on one local interval common to all cutoffs. The same
  spectral-weight gap controls the initial tail and the discarded nonlinear
  source. Stability and a common observation modulus then give the least cutoff
  certified by the estimate. Conditions on the interaction support determine which
  exponential reserves propagate without loss. Single-mode tests make the analytic
  threshold sharp in the Neumann and Stokes realizations. Compatible
  approximations of the source and time integrals preserve generated presentations
  along the local flow. Quadratic observations carry a complementary account of
  retained energy and transport defect together with signed injection and
  approximation increments. The cross terms are kept throughout composition and
  passage to limits. History-resolved Neumann readouts give response bounds
  without a multiplicity factor and identify the invariance condition for closed
  evolution of the observed values. The realizations have explicit constants. The
  Neumann source is derived within the paper under the stated composition and
  scaling assumptions with the endpoint and reflecting conditions.
keywords:
  - "Nonlinear evolution equations"
  - "Spectral Galerkin approximation"
  - "Gevrey regularity"
  - "Analytic semigroups"
